data.content:
# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `SetProperty` Expression Trees — Value Must Be a Lambda

EF Core 10 unified `SetProperty` overloads so the **value** argument is always `Expression<Func<TSource, TProperty>>`, not a bare expression. Your `Expression.Constant("foo")` breaks.

**Fix:** Wrap the value in a lambda:

```csharp
var blogParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b");
var valueParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b2"); // separate parameter

setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyCalls<Blog>, SetPropertyCalls<Blog>>>(
    Expression.Call(
        instance: setters.Body,
        methodName: nameof(SetPropertyCalls<Blog>.SetProperty),
        typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) },
        arguments: new Expression[]
        {
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
            // ↓ NOW a lambda instead of Expression.Constant
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Constant("foo"), valueParameter)
        }),
    setters.Parameters);
```

---

## 2. Complex Types — Column Naming Now Prefixed

EF Core 10 prefixes complex-property columns with the navigation name to eliminate ambiguity (e.g., `ShippingAddress_Street` and `BillingAddress_Street` instead of two `Street` columns colliding).

Existing databases will get a migration that **renames columns**. If you want to preserve the old names:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, a =>
    {
        a.Property(x => x.Street).HasColumnName("Street");
        // repeat for each property…
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, a =>
    {
        a.Property(x => x.Street).HasColumnName("BillingStreet");
    });
});

// For nested: Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City
// New default column: "Payment_BillingAddress_City"
// Pin the old name with .HasColumnName(...) if needed.
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — New `CommandSource` Parameter

EF Core 10 added a `CommandSource` parameter to the diagnostics methods so consumers can distinguish query vs. migration vs. SaveChanges commands.

**Fix:**

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // ← add this
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

---

## Summary

| Area | What broke | One-line fix |
|------|-----------|--------------|
| `SetProperty` expr trees | Value arg must be a lambda | Wrap constant in `Expression.Lambda<Func<T,TProp>>(…)` |
| Complex types | Column names now include nav prefix | Pin old names via `HasColumnName()` or accept new migration |
| Diagnostics logger | `CommandSource` param added | Add the parameter to your implementation |
